menu "Station: availability ledger"

    config LEDGER_TARGET_PERMILLE
        int "Daily graced-uptime target (per mille)"
        range 900 1000
        default 998
        help
            Graced uptime the protected (Onocoy) slot needs per UTC day.
            Onocoy's streak needs Availability >= 0.98, which on its scoring
            curve means a graced uptime of 99.8 %: with the daily grant below
            that is about 473 s of downtime per UTC day at a perfect data
            rate (a plain 98 % uptime would score only about 0.81).

    config LEDGER_GRACE_S
        int "Daily downtime grant of the protected slot (s)"
        range 0 3600
        default 300
        help
            Seconds Onocoy adds to each day's uptime before scoring it.

    config LEDGER_HISTORY_DAYS
        int "Days of per-day history kept"
        range 7 90
        default 30

    config LEDGER_PLANNED_BOOT_S
        int "After a planned reboot, count a caster's reconnect as planned for at most (s)"
        range 0 600
        default 120
        help
            A reboot the user asked for (restart, app update or rollback,
            factory reset, leaving safe mode) is planned downtime: the gap
            until the next boot, and each caster's time after the boot until
            it streams again, at most this long after the boot. A normal
            boot streams again within about 20-40 s (boot, Wi-Fi association
            and DHCP, the receiver keeps streaming, one caster connect and
            perhaps one quick retry while the caster lets go of the old
            session); 120 s leaves room for a slow access point. A caster
            still down after that has a problem of its own (Wi-Fi, internet,
            the caster), which counts as unplanned like after any other
            boot. The downtime counts against the daily budget either way;
            this only decides how much of it is shown as planned. 0 = only
            the gap until the boot.

    config LEDGER_ALIVE_PERSIST_S
        int "Persist the last-alive mark every (s)"
        range 10 600
        default 60
        help
            After a power cut, the time between this mark and the next boot
            is counted as downtime. When today's counters changed since the
            last store (some slot was down, or the protected slot lost
            epochs, in the meantime) they are stored with the mark as one
            record, in one write; otherwise the mark alone is written.
            Shorter is more exact but writes NVS more often: at 60 s the
            mark alone is about 1500 page erases per page in 10 years with
            the 112 KB NVS partition, and the record stored at every mark
            for years (a slot that stays down, or lost epochs every
            interval) makes it about six times that (the record is six NVS
            entries, the mark alone one), still well inside the flash's
            rating.

endmenu
